+## Rules
+1. **One CVSS version, 3.1, for every finding.** Two CPTC10 finalists mixed versions within one report, and it reads as careless.
+2. **Rate what we demonstrated, not what is theoretically possible.** Every finding gets a one-line "Why this severity" in its table.
+3. **Hygiene items default to Low or Informational** unless we demonstrated impact. That covers missing headers, verbose errors, policy recommendations and broken functionality. Inflating them costs credibility; CPTC10 judges praised a team for downplaying insignificant issues.
+4. **Always write severity as a word.** Color is a supplement and never carries the meaning alone.
+
 ## Context adjustments (state them in the finding)
-- **Raise** for: guest physical safety (rides), payment data (PCI DSS), being a step in a demonstrated chain to full compromise.
-- **Lower** for: requires unlikely preconditions, or compensating controls were observed.
+- **Raise** for guest physical safety (rides) and payment data (PCI DSS).
+- **Raise a link in a demonstrated attack chain only if it is a choke point**, meaning that fixing it alone breaks the path to the critical asset. Show the combined impact in the report's Attack Path section instead of raising every link.
+- **Lower** when exploitation requires unlikely preconditions or we observed compensating controls.
+
+## Calibration examples (CPTC10 finals, same environment)
+| Issue | How finalists rated it | Lesson |
+|---|---|---|
+| Unauthenticated custom code interpreter (RCE) | Medium 4.2 up to Critical 9.8 | Part of the spread is legitimate: one team only showed a file write, while others got root. Rate what you proved, and say what you proved. |
+| Brute-forceable TOTP on the web app | Critical (two teams) and Low (two teams) | Without a stated rationale the report reads as arbitrary. Write the "why". |
 
 ## Consistency rule
 The lead assigns the final severity for every finding, so the report reads as one voice.
