+# Team Tracker
+
+These are five CSV files that together give the team one shared picture of the engagement. Open them in LibreOffice Calc (preinstalled on Kali) or any text editor. The design follows what finalist teams built as web apps (PSUT's jVision, Tennessee Tech's R3CON), but uses plain files: we need the function, not another app to debug on the day.
+
+**Setup at kickoff:** copy this folder to a location the whole team can reach inside the competition environment. **The live copies never come back into this repo.** The repo is public, and these files will hold real credentials and client data.
+
+**Editing:** each person updates only their own rows. The lead owns `findings-index.csv`. Save often, and don't keep a file open in two editors at once.
+
+| File | One row per | Columns worth knowing |
+|---|---|---|
+| `hosts.csv` | Host | `stage`: `todo` → `enum` → `foothold` → `privesc` → `owned`, or `ot-do-not-touch` (also add those to `ot-hosts.txt`). `owner` is who is working it. |
+| `credentials.csv` | Credential | `source_finding` is where it came from. `tried_on` / `works_on` list hosts or services, so no one retries blindly or locks an account. In the report, redact `secret` to its first and last characters. |
+| `created-accounts-and-artifacts.csv` | Anything we added or changed on a system: accounts, files, services, config changes | `cleaned_up` = `yes` / `no`. This is the cleanup list and the source for report Appendix F. For files, put the SHA-256 in `item`. |
+| `attack-path.csv` | Edge in the attack path (`from` → `to`) | `choke_point` = `yes` when fixing that one finding breaks the path. This becomes the Attack Path diagram in the report. |
+| `findings-index.csv` | Finding | The lead's master list of IDs. `status`: `drafting` / `review` / `done`. QA checks the report against this file. |
